WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.152
Your digestive system breaks food into small pieces your body can take in and use. Enzymes help this breakdown.

00:00:07.152 --> 00:00:14.919
Follow a meal from mouth to anus. The liver, gallbladder and pancreas add liquids, but food does not pass through them.

00:00:14.919 --> 00:00:21.292
Teeth grind food. Your tongue mixes it with saliva, then forms a soft ball called a bolus.

00:00:21.292 --> 00:00:28.983
Salivary glands make saliva. It wets food and contains amylase, which starts breaking starch into smaller sugars.

00:00:28.983 --> 00:00:35.254
Tongue glands make lingual lipase. This enzyme helps digest fat, mainly in the stomach.

00:00:35.254 --> 00:00:41.680
Your tongue pushes food into the throat. A flap called the epiglottis helps keep food out of the windpipe.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:50.846
Muscles squeeze food down the food pipe, or oesophagus. These squeezing waves are called peristalsis. No new enzymes are added here.

00:00:50.846 --> 00:01:00.269
The stomach mixes food into a thick liquid called chyme. Acid helps pepsin work. Pepsin cuts proteins into smaller pieces called peptides.

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:07.983
Acid is not an enzyme. It stops salivary amylase working. Gastric and lingual lipases digest some fat.

00:01:07.983 --> 00:01:14.700
Mucus protects the stomach wall. The stomach also makes intrinsic factor, a protein needed later.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:22.698
Food enters the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine. The pancreas adds enzymes for starch, protein and fat.

00:01:22.698 --> 00:01:26.268
It also adds bicarbonate to make the mixture less acidic.

00:01:26.268 --> 00:01:35.607
The liver makes bile. The gallbladder stores and releases it. Bile breaks large fat drops into smaller drops. It is not an enzyme.

00:01:35.607 --> 00:01:42.718
Pancreatic amylase continues breaking starch into smaller sugar pieces. Surface enzymes will finish the job.

00:01:42.718 --> 00:01:50.632
We cannot make cellulase, the enzyme that breaks down cellulose in plant cell walls. This fibre moves towards the colon.

00:01:50.632 --> 00:01:56.336
The pancreas sends protein-digesting enzymes in a switched-off form, helping protect itself.

00:01:56.336 --> 00:02:03.806
An intestinal enzyme called enteropeptidase switches on trypsin. Trypsin switches on other protein-digesting enzymes.

00:02:03.806 --> 00:02:13.910
Trypsin, chymotrypsin and elastase cut protein chains. Carboxypeptidase removes amino acids, the building blocks of protein, from the ends.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:22.585
Pancreatic lipase breaks fat into fatty acids and smaller fat pieces called monoglycerides. A protein called colipase helps.

00:02:22.585 --> 00:02:29.843
Phospholipase and cholesterol esterase act on other fats. Bile helps bring fat pieces to the intestinal wall.

00:02:29.843 --> 00:02:34.772
RNase and DNase break down genetic material from food cells.

00:02:34.772 --> 00:02:42.190
Surface enzymes finish digestion. Maltase changes maltose into glucose, a simple sugar used for energy.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:53.056
Sucrase acts on table sugar. Lactase acts on milk sugar. Glucoamylase and isomaltase finish starch pieces. Trehalase breaks down trehalose.

00:02:53.056 --> 00:02:58.034
Aminopeptidases and dipeptidases turn protein pieces into amino acids.

00:02:58.034 --> 00:03:06.748
Nucleotidases, phosphatases and nucleosidases finish breaking down genetic material. The products are small enough for absorption.

00:03:06.748 --> 00:03:14.723
Absorption means taking digested food through the intestinal wall into the body. Tiny finger-like projections called villi cover this wall.

00:03:14.723 --> 00:03:20.680
Each villus has a thin covering, tiny blood vessels, and a central lymph tube called a lacteal.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:28.070
Smaller projections called microvilli cover each absorptive cell. They increase the surface area for taking in nutrients.

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:34.018
Simple sugars and amino acids pass into tiny blood vessels. Blood takes them to the liver.

00:03:34.018 --> 00:03:40.067
Most long-chain fat pieces are packed into tiny bundles called chylomicrons inside intestinal cells.

00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:47.940
These bundles enter lacteals, travel in lymph, then join the blood. Some shorter fatty acids enter blood directly.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:55.978
Water, vitamins and minerals are also absorbed. Cells use nutrients for energy, growth and repair: assimilation.

00:03:55.978 --> 00:04:00.584
Much absorption happens in the jejunum. It continues in the ileum.

00:04:00.584 --> 00:04:09.710
The last part of the ileum absorbs vitamin B12 with intrinsic factor, the protein made in the stomach. It also recovers bile salts for reuse.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.704
Remaining material enters the large intestine.

00:04:12.704 --> 00:04:17.545
The colon takes in more water and salts, making the remaining material firmer.

00:04:17.545 --> 00:04:26.645
Helpful bacteria use their enzymes to break down some fibre. They make small fatty acids, including butyrate, which feeds colon cells.

00:04:26.645 --> 00:04:33.588
These bacteria make vitamin K and some B vitamins. They help protect the gut, but also produce gas.

00:04:33.588 --> 00:04:38.217
Undigested food, bacteria, old cells and water form faeces.

00:04:38.217 --> 00:04:44.492
The rectum stores faeces. Rings of muscle called anal sphincters control release through the anus.

00:04:44.492 --> 00:04:53.627
This is egestion: passing out undigested and unabsorbed material. Excretion instead removes wastes made by body cells, such as urea.

00:04:53.627 --> 00:05:00.000
Remember: ingestion, digestion, absorption, assimilation, then egestion.
